        <h4 class="bb-h">Then it all had to work together.</h4>
        <p class="bb-body">
          The hard part was integration: the dial, the matrix, the touch pad and the motor in one loop, and two boxes that each have to send
          and listen at once. This is one exchange, as the code runs it on both ends.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bb-sys">
        <ol class="bb-seq" data-bb-seq>
          <li class="bb-lane" aria-hidden="true">Your box</li>
          <li class="bb-lane" aria-hidden="true">Friend’s box</li>
          {
            seq.map((s) => (
              <li class:list={['bb-ev', `is-${s.side}`, s.dir && `to-${s.dir}`]}>
                {s.side === 'msg' ? (
                  <span class="bb-msg">
                    <code>{s.t}</code>
                    <i aria-hidden="true" />
                  </span>
                ) : (
                  <>
                    <b>{s.t}</b>
                    <code>{s.d}</code>
                  </>
                )}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <figure class="bb-loop">
          <pre><code><span class="k">while</span> True:
    check_message()       <span class="c"># anything from the other box?</span>
    button.update()       <span class="c"># the dial's push switch</span>
    touch_button.update() <span class="c"># one tap or two</span>

    val_new = r.value()   <span class="c"># where the dial is</span>
    <span class="k">if</span> val_old != val_new:
        ...               <span class="c"># redraw the icon</span>

    sleep_ms(<span class="n">10</span>)</code></pre>
          <figcaption>The heart of finalcodeciid.py. Every ten milliseconds, each box listens, reads its controls and redraws.</figcaption>
        </figure>
